System Architecture

Overview

The US Treasury AI Data Analyst Agent is an n8n workflow that retrieves federal financial data from NocoDB, filters records for fiscal year 2025, ranks agencies by net cost, validates financial totals, generates a chart, and emails a report.

Workflow Pipeline

Trigger — The workflow can start manually or through a weekly schedule.

Data Retrieval — An HTTP Request node retrieves records from NocoDB.

Record Expansion — Split Out converts the returned records into individual items.

Fiscal-Year Filtering — The Filter node selects FY2025 records.

Field Mapping and Ranking — JavaScript and Edit Fields nodes prepare financial fields and order records by net cost.

Top Five Selection — The Limit node retains five records for the ranked report.

Financial Validation — JavaScript checks whether gross cost minus earned revenue reconciles with net cost, using a tolerance of 0.1 billion.

Quality Summary — The workflow counts records that pass validation and records that require review.

Chart Generation — A QuickChart HTTP request creates a chart image from the selected records.

Report Assembly and Delivery — Merge nodes combine report, quality-summary, and chart outputs before the Gmail node sends the email.

Technology Stack

n8n Cloud — Workflow orchestration and scheduling

NocoDB — Data storage and API access

JavaScript — Sorting, transformation, and data-quality checks

QuickChart — Chart image generation

Gmail — Email delivery

Google Gemini — Optional AI Agent component with chat memory

Data-Quality Logic

For each record, the workflow calculates:

reconciliation_difference = gross_cost - earned_revenue - net_cost

A record is flagged when values are missing or invalid, or when the absolute reconciliation difference exceeds 0.1 billion. A flag indicates that a record needs review; it does not by itself prove that the source data is incorrect.

Operational Notes

The workflow is configured for a weekly schedule.

The current report focuses on FY2025 and the five highest net-cost records.

Gemini availability can depend on API quotas and account limits.

Credentials must be configured securely in n8n and must not be committed to source control.

After importing the public workflow JSON, users must replace placeholders and configure their own credentials.

Limitations

This workflow uses the records returned by the configured NocoDB view and request settings. Results depend on source-data quality, the selected fiscal year, and the records available to the workflow. The data-quality checks are reconciliation checks, not a complete audit of federal financial statements.

